	affiliations = {National University of Science and Technology POLITEHNICA Bucharest, Computer Science Department, Bucharest, Romania},
	abstract = {Starting with the pandemic, and then amplified by the launch of publicly available large language models like ChatGPT, which are capable to generate snippets of code for different tasks described in natural language, there is an increased concern in academia regarding code plagiarism as a means for students to achieve academic success by completing faculty-related projects and practical examinations.Throughout the time, multiple solutions were proposed to detect plagiarism in code, but there were issues regarding the different types of plagiarism these algorithms were able to detect. In this paper, we propose to start with a detailed analysis of the most influential plagiarism detection tools used in academic setups, identifying the core principles and effective strategies that could serve as the groundwork for developing a new state-of-the-art algorithm.The purpose of our research is to investigate the foundations of five of the most widely used state-of-the-art code plagiarism detection algorithms and to evaluated these tools on a real-world dataset comprised of 131 source code files written in Java, comparing their behavior against different obfuscation techniques.  © 2025 IEEE.},
