	abstract = {The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into education presents a pedagogical paradox. This paper introduces the Socratic Tutor for Adaptive Programming (STAP), a system that facilitates guided discovery rather than direct answers. We reframe the LLM's role from an "oracle"to a "Socratic tutor"via pedagogical scaffolding. STAP employs a four-stage pipeline - Checking, Correcting, Complementing, Segmenting - to analyze student code in real time. We detail design and implementation through an example, followed by a scope-limited formative evaluation providing feasibility signals. We also formalize key concepts (Socratic hints, MVH, and answer leakage). Findings indicate adherence to pedagogical principles and motivate classroom studies. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
