	affiliations = {School of Computer Science, Zhuhai College of Science and Technology, Guangdong, Zhuhai, China},
	abstract = {Compiler error messages are often cryptic and challenging for novices, especially in C++. Existing approaches, such as FAQ lists, enhanced compiler error messages, and large language models (LLMs), provide partial support but remain insufficient for cultivating the skills needed to resolve compiler errors. This study presents a student-centered Compiler Error Solution Query Platform, designed under outcome-based education principles. The platform integrates LLM-generated and instructor-authored solutions, employs a dual-stage error classification process, and associates error categories to course knowledge points. It supports rapid error resolution, personalized knowledge accumulation, and data-driven teaching feedback. Across two semesters, the system captured over 10,000 error queries, filtered into 2,500+ distinct errors and further mapped into 210+ error patterns, which were then classified into seven categories. The results demonstrate that integrating LLM automation with human expertise offers scalable and pedagogically grounded support, bridging the gap between immediate error correction and the cultivation of transferable problem-solving skills. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
