	abstract = {The assessment of programming assignments in high school faces challenges with efficiency and feedback quality. Manual grading is slow and subjective, while conventional autograders typically only evaluate functional correctness, neglecting qualitative aspects like code readability and good programming practices. This research investigates the use of Large Language Models (LLMs), specifically Google Gemini, to develop a comprehensive automated system that provides both objective scores and in-depth, human-like qualitative feedback. A prototype was developed to analyze C++ tasks and evaluated through a direct comparative study, benchmarking Gemini's results against an expert teacher's assessment. Findings indicate Gemini has significant potential as a reliable tool, accurately identifying functional errors while also providing relevant insights into code quality. The novelty lies in its holistic approach beyond correctness and its pedagogical validation against a human expert. This Gemini-based system can bridge the gap between machine objectivity and human feedback, significantly enhancing programming education.  © 2025 IEEE.},
