	affiliations = {Hame University of Applied Sciences, Hameenlinna, Finland},
	abstract = {This work evaluated how well a large language model can solve programming exercises in online courses, presenting the results in the form of an interactive dashboard. We focused on seven free and public Finnish MOOCs in introductory programming. The dataset contained 741 exercises in different programming languages. The OpenAI o1 model was asked to solve the tasks, and we recorded whether it succeeded. In total, the model solved 721 tasks, giving an overall success rate of 97.3%. Performance was consistent across Java and Python, and somewhat lower in Rust. While most exercises were solved easily, larger multi-step problems required repeated prompting or additional context. To visualize the findings and spark discussion, we created an interactive dashboard with several different views for the data. We suggest that such tools may help teachers, researchers, and students discuss how well AI "synthetic students"perform in MOOCs, and reflect on how this affects the pedagogy.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
